# Observed

Read-only console capture of cmd PID 45180, cwd G:/ciel-Walkie, shows:
`RuntimeError: wake attempt ownership persistence rejected`.
The frame chain is direct terminal polling -> pending input injection ->
ChannelWakeDeliveryRepository.begin_attempt ->
RuntimeInputStatusRepository.begin_attempt, line 107.

Router instance 9505-aae7db361c7f records the same failure at local time
2026-10-20T14:43:56, followed by direct-subprocess fallback and exit code 1.
The affected source path is the 9ac82f0 snapshot. User registration now points
to f6fc7ac, but both installed runtime_input_status.py files have identical
SHA-256 16934830ed1cd059b2e30a67fbc3b0d9cf6a017c965521f36113e852251641cd.
No claim is made that selecting the new version alone fixes this rejection.

# Evidence

- terminal-traceback.txt: captured native console text, without keyboard input.
- log-markers.json: bounded crash markers from this workspace's router log.
- status-summary.json: metadata-only status rows near cursor 11901; no payloads.
- screenshot-attempt.txt: console HWND rectangle is 0,0,0,0, preventing direct
  console-window screenshot capture. Terminal text remains available.

# Unknown

The rejected message id and preceding state must be established before changing
ownership or retry behavior. The new crash is not the prior codex-remote alias
KeyError. No native crash from the new f6fc7ac code was observed in this capture.

# Safety

No terminal input, restart, queue deletion, cursor advancement, configuration
change, or old-snapshot overwrite was performed. A malformed command probe
owned by this investigation was terminated after verifying its exact argv.


# Implementation and reproduced defect

The rejected input id in the Walkie incident is unknown; cursor 11901 is not
proof that 11901 was rejected. Independent isolated reproduction demonstrated
that durable replied/skipped rows with missing transcript evidence reach the
ownership guard. A completion racing selection also reproduced the conflict;
a mixed batch previously wrote earlier queued members before the conflict.

Celly sandbox G:/ciel-bridge-service router.log.1 independently records the same
begin_attempt frame at 2026-10-20T17:40:25 and a routed exit diagnostic at
17:40:28 explicitly identifying f6fc7ac. Therefore the defect is also observed on
the new installed build, not only a stale Walkie process. No sandbox state was
changed; its status file read was denied (WinError 5).

Changed candidate selection to exclude durable replied/skipped before transport
gates, retain existing failed/uncertain handling, and preserve submitted owners
without replay. Batch admission now locks across repository instances and
preflights the full batch before writing any member. A typed pre-send conflict
releases this scan's claims and defers to a bounded poll without cursor or marker
advance. Generic errors and post-send conflicts are not silently retried.
Existing ownership cannot be stolen even by a new epoch. Partial I/O failure is
fail-closed, not claimed to be transactional rollback.

# Focused verification

72 focused ownership/poll/injection tests: zero failures/errors. Includes bound
child epochs, missing transcripts, mixed-batch racing completion, rebuilt prompt,
no duplicate send, preserved cursor floor, cross-instance contention, strict
corrupt history, and non-retryable failure after a partial ownership write.
306 adjacent tests: zero failures/errors, 80 skipped. Bridge fixtures required
per-test durable status isolation because synthetic ids were reused across
cases. The initial adjacent run had 8 failures from this shared history; the
fixture correction and passing rerun are retained separately. Ruff passed.
The final adjacent evidence is in `final-adjacent.txt`.
Native owned acceptance `run-claude-ownership-fix-174500` passed scoped restart delivery: old native gone, new input replied, old submitted inputs fenced, and no listeners remained after teardown. The harness reported app-server listener release as not applicable for Claude. Live Walkie/Celly sessions were not restarted.
